{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And he shall bring upon the sacrifice of the thanksgiving offering</b> — they explained it as meaning \"with the sacrifice of the thanksgiving offering,\" and likewise \"upon cakes of leavened bread\" — meaning \"with them.\" But the correct interpretation is that עַל means what it sounds like: that he adds bread upon the sacrifice. And likewise, \"upon cakes of leavened bread he shall bring his offering\" — that he shall bring cakes of leavened bread and upon them he shall additionally bring his offering, namely the unleavened bread mentioned, and he shall bring everything upon the sacrifice. And Scripture made the leavened bread the principal and the rest of the bread secondary to it, because it is the most numerous, corresponding to all the other types. And the verse repeated, saying \"upon the sacrifice of his peace-offering of thanksgiving,\" for what the Sages derived from it (Menahot 78a): that the bread does not attain consecration as consecration of the body until the sacrifice is slaughtered and slaughtered upon it — that is, on condition that the bread is consecrated along with it. And Rashi wrote (Rashi on Leviticus 7:12): \"And he shall bring upon the sacrifice of the thanksgiving offering\" — four types of bread: loaves and wafers and boiled dough, being three types of unleavened bread; and it is written \"upon cakes of leavened bread\"; and each type consists of ten loaves; so it is explained in Menahot (77b); and their measure is five Jerusalem se'ah, which are six wilderness measures, twenty tenths. And the Rav did not mention these twenty tenths, from which ten go to leavened bread — one tenth per loaf — and ten to unleavened bread — three loaves per tenth — for the Sages derived this (there, 77b) from this verse: \"upon cakes of leavened bread\" — the leavened brought parallels the unleavened, so it works out to twenty tenths: ten for leavened bread and ten for unleavened bread; ten for leavened bread — one tenth per loaf, and ten for unleavened bread — three loaves per tenth; so it works out to forty loaves."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The Rav (Maimonides) is cited in this passage; the reference is apparently to a discussion in Mishneh Torah, though the specific locus is not identified in the Hebrew text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:51:20.571726967Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}